Under an <orthogonal matrix> $R$, the <Levi-Civita symbol> satisfies
$$
R_{ia}R_{jb}R_{mc}\epsilon_{abc}=(\det R)\epsilon_{ijm}.
$$
Use this twice, and use $R_{mc}R_{mf}=\delta_{cf}$ for the contracted index. It follows that
$$
R_{ia}R_{jb}R_{kc}R_{ld}\epsilon_{abe}\epsilon_{cde}
=(\det R)^2\epsilon_{ijm}\epsilon_{klm}=T_{ijkl}.
$$
Therefore $\boxed{T'_{ijkl}=R_{ia}R_{jb}R_{kc}R_{ld}T_{abcd}=T_{ijkl}}$: it is a fourth-rank <isotropic tensor>. Although a single alternating symbol is a <pseudotensor> under reflections, the two determinant factors multiply to one, so this product is an ordinary <tensor> even for improper orthogonal transformations.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use three components of the proposed <isotropic tensor> form. Since $T_{1122}=0$, one gets $\alpha=0$. Since $T_{1212}=\epsilon_{123}^2=1$, one gets $\beta=1$. Finally $T_{1221}=\epsilon_{123}\epsilon_{213}=-1$ gives $\gamma=-1$. Hence <contraction of two Levi-Civita symbols> gives
$$
\boxed{\epsilon_{ijm}\epsilon_{klm}=\delta_{ik}\delta_{jl}-\delta_{il}\delta_{jk},\qquad(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)=(0,1,-1).}
$$

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The integration domain is invariant under every <rotation matrix>, and its volume element is unchanged. The substitution $\mathbf y=R\mathbf x$ therefore proves
$$
R_{ia}R_{jb}R_{kc}R_{ld}I_{abcd}=\int_B y_iy_jy_ky_l\,dV_{\mathbf y}=I_{ijkl}.
$$
Thus $I$ is an <isotropic tensor>. It is also completely symmetric in its four indices, because the coordinate factors commute. In the general isotropic form, exchanging indices $j,k$ equates $\alpha$ and $\beta$, and exchanging $j,l$ equates $\alpha$ and $\gamma$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{I_{ijkl}=A(\delta_{ij}\delta_{kl}+\delta_{ik}\delta_{jl}+\delta_{il}\delta_{jk}),\qquad\alpha=\beta=\gamma=A.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The contraction in the PDF is $I_{iijj}$. By <Einstein notation>, its integrand is $(\sum_i x_i^2)(\sum_jx_j^2)=r^4$. In <spherical coordinates>,
$$
\boxed{I_{iijj}=\int_Br^4\,dV=4\pi\int_0^1r^6\,dr=\frac{4\pi}{7}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Contract the isotropic expression from (i). Since $\delta_{ii}=3$ and $\delta_{ij}\delta_{ij}=3$,
$$
I_{iijj}=A(9+3+3)=15A.
$$
The value in (ii) gives $A=4\pi/105$, so the <isotropic fourth moment of a ball> is
$$
\boxed{I_{ijkl}=\frac{4\pi}{105}(\delta_{ij}\delta_{kl}+\delta_{ik}\delta_{jl}+\delta_{il}\delta_{jk}).}
$$
For example $\int_Bx_1^4\,dV=4\pi/35$ and $\int_Bx_1^2x_2^2\,dV=4\pi/105$, illustrating the factor of three between these two fourth moments.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Write the inverse <tensor> as
$$
Y_{ij}=\delta_{ij}+\lambda A_{ij}+\lambda^2C_{ij}+\lambda^3D_{ij}+O(\lambda^4).
$$
Insert this into $(\delta_{ik}+\lambda X_{ik})Y_{kj}=\delta_{ij}$ and equate powers of $\lambda$. One obtains successively
$$
A_{ij}=-X_{ij},\qquad C_{ij}=-X_{ik}A_{kj}=X_{ik}X_{kj},\qquad
D_{ij}=-X_{ik}C_{kj}=-X_{ik}X_{kl}X_{lj}.
$$
Thus the <Neumann series> gives
$$
\boxed{Y_{ij}=\delta_{ij}-\lambda X_{ij}+\lambda^2X_{ik}X_{kj}-\lambda^3X_{ik}X_{kl}X_{lj}+O(\lambda^4).}
$$
Every contraction is an ordinary ordered <matrix product>. No transpose or symmetry assumption is used. Multiplying the truncated series on either side by $I+\lambda X$ leaves $I-\lambda^4X^4$, which verifies the error order. For a fixed finite matrix this expansion converges whenever $|\lambda|\,\|X\|<1$ in a submultiplicative <matrix norm>.

