= Solution
{parent=a}

For the stated square side length $2a$, the volume is $4a^2h$ and the uniform <mass density> is $\rho_1=m_1/(4a^2h)$. Direct integration about its central vertical axis gives the <moment of inertia of a uniform square plate>:
$$
I_1=\rho_1h\int_{-a}^a\int_{-a}^a(x^2+y^2)\,dx\,dy
=\rho_1h\frac{8a^4}{3}
=\boxed{\frac23m_1a^2}.
$$
Thus the PDF's coefficient $1/6$ is inconsistent with its stated side length. The coefficient $1/6$ would apply to a square of side $a$.

For the disc, use <cylindrical coordinates> and $m_2=\rho_2\pi a^2h$:
$$
I_2=\rho_2h\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^a r^2r\,dr\,d\theta
=\frac{\pi\rho_2ha^4}{2}=\boxed{\frac12m_2a^2}.
$$
For the sphere, use <spherical coordinates> and $m_3=4\pi\rho_3a^3/3$:
$$
I_3=\rho_3\int_0^a r^4dr\int_0^\pi\sin^3\theta\,d\theta\int_0^{2\pi}d\phi
=\frac{8\pi\rho_3a^5}{15}=\boxed{\frac25m_3a^2}.
$$
These agree with the <moment of inertia of a uniform disc> and the <moment of inertia of a uniform solid sphere>. The square's thickness does not enter its axial <moment of inertia> once the mass is fixed.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Measure each <generalized coordinate> $\theta_i$ from an unstressed configuration. The diagram has one <torsional spring> joining the frame to object 1, one joining objects 1 and 2, and one joining objects 2 and 3. The <rotational kinetic energy> and <elastic potential energy> are
$$
T=\frac12\sum_{i=1}^3I_i\dot\theta_i^2,\qquad
U=\frac12k_1\theta_1^2+\frac12k_2(\theta_2-\theta_1)^2+\frac12k_3(\theta_3-\theta_2)^2.
$$
Each spring contribution follows by integrating its restoring <torque>: $\int_0^{\Delta\theta}k_j s\,ds=k_j(\Delta\theta)^2/2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{L=T-U.}
$$
The <Euler-Lagrange equations> give
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
I_1\ddot\theta_1+(k_1+k_2)\theta_1-k_2\theta_2&=0,\\
I_2\ddot\theta_2-k_2\theta_1+(k_2+k_3)\theta_2-k_3\theta_3&=0,\\
I_3\ddot\theta_3-k_3\theta_2+k_3\theta_3&=0.
\end{aligned}}
$$
These hold for either interpretation of the square's <moment of inertia>; the geometry-based value from (a) is used first below.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the stated geometry and numerical masses, the inertia and <stiffness matrices> are
$$
M=\operatorname{diag}\left(2,\frac14,\frac2{25}\right),\qquad
K=\begin{pmatrix}5&-2&0\\-2&3&-1\\0&-1&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
A <normal mode> $\boldsymbol\theta=\mathbf v e^{i\omega t}$ requires $(K-\omega^2M)\mathbf v=0$. Writing $z=\omega^2$, its <characteristic equation> is
$$
\det(K-zM)=-\frac{4z^3-108z^2+613z-600}{100}=0.
$$
At the claimed $z=6$, this determinant is $-27/50\ne0$. Hence **the printed claim that $\sqrt6$ is a normal frequency is false for the stated data**, and there is no corresponding nonzero <normal mode>.

The complete geometry-based answer is obtained from the three positive roots $z_j$:
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
4z_j^3-108z_j^2+613z_j-600&=0,\\
(\omega_{\rm low},\omega_{\rm middle},\omega_{\rm high})
&\simeq(1.111471775,\ 2.491790287,\ 4.422172730).
\end{aligned}}
$$
An exact closed form for the squared <angular frequencies> is
$$
z_j=9+\sqrt{\frac{359}{3}}\cos\left[\frac13\arccos\frac{2745\sqrt3}{359^{3/2}}-\frac{2\pi j}{3}\right],\qquad j=0,1,2.
$$
They are approximately $19.555611657$, $6.209018836$ and $1.235369507$, respectively. For any of these roots, a corresponding <eigenvector> can be chosen as
$$
\boxed{\mathbf v(z)=\begin{pmatrix}
\tfrac12\left[(3-z/4)(1-2z/25)-1\right]\\
1-2z/25\\
1
\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The last two rows of $(K-zM)\mathbf v=0$ give this expression directly; the first is the characteristic equation. These three modes span all motion, with arbitrary sine and cosine amplitudes. Positivity of $M$ and of the spring <potential energy> ensures that all squared <angular frequencies> are positive.

If instead one takes the PDF's printed $I_1=m_1a^2/6$ as a stipulated inertia, then $M=\operatorname{diag}(1/2,1/4,2/25)$ and
$$
\det(K-zM)=-\frac{2z^3-69z^2+626z-1200}{200}.
$$
The corresponding <angular frequencies> are approximately
$$
\boxed{(1.616170898,\ 3.315716400,\ 4.570997307).}
$$
The same formula for $\mathbf v(z)$ applies, because its derivation used only the second and third rows. Here the determinant at $z=6$ is $-63/25\ne0$, so following the printed inertia does not repair the frequency claim. The PDF therefore needs a further correction to its numerical data or asserted frequency; the intended correction cannot be inferred uniquely.

