= Solution
{parent=i}

Subtraction fails <associativity>: $(0*0)*1=-1$ but $0*(0*1)=1$. Thus it violates the <group axioms> and is **not a group**. It has a right identity $0$, but no element can be a two-sided <identity element>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The operation is not defined for every pair in the proposed set: choosing $a=1,b=-1$ makes its denominator zero. Thus it is not even a <binary operation> on $\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$, and is **not a group**. In particular, removing zero from the domain does not prevent the sum in the denominator from vanishing.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The substitution $F(a)=e^a$, with $F(-\infty)=0$, is a <bijection> onto $[0,\infty)$ and obeys
$$
F(a*b)=F(a)+F(b).
$$
Thus the <log-sum-exp function> transfers nonnegative addition to the given set. It is closed, associative and commutative, with <identity element> $-\infty$. But a finite $a$ has $e^a>0$, and adding a nonnegative number cannot give zero, so no finite $a$ has an <inverse element>. Therefore it is a <commutative monoid> but **not a group**.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The sum of two <odd integers> plus one is odd, so the operation is closed. Both associations of a triple give $a+b+c+2$, proving <associativity>. Its <identity element> is $-1$, and the <inverse element> of $a$ is $-a-2$, also odd, since $a+(-a-2)+1=-1$. Thus it is an <abelian group>.

More explicitly, <shifted addition on odd integers> is ordinary integer addition in different coordinates:
$$
\boxed{F(a)=\frac{a+1}{2},\qquad F(a*b)=F(a)+F(b).}
$$
This <bijection> is a <group isomorphism> to $(\mathbb Z,+)$.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <subgroup> $H\leq G$ and $g\in G$, the left and right <cosets> are $gH=\{gh:h\in H\}$ and $Hg=\{hg:h\in H\}$. A <normal subgroup> satisfies $gHg^{-1}=H$ for every $g$. Multiplying this set equality on the right by $g$ gives
$$
\boxed{gH=Hg\quad\text{for every }g\in G.}
$$
Conversely this equality implies $gHg^{-1}=H$, so it is an equivalent definition of normality.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a <normal subgroup> $H$, the <quotient group> consists of its <cosets> with multiplication
$$
\boxed{G/H=\{gH:g\in G\},\qquad(gH)(kH)=(gk)H.}
$$
To check that this is well-defined, replace $g,k$ by $gh_1,kh_2$ with $h_1,h_2\in H$. Their product is
$$
gh_1kh_2=gk(k^{-1}h_1k)h_2\in gkH,
$$
since normality puts $k^{-1}h_1k$ in $H$. Hence the product does not depend on representatives. <Associativity> follows from that in $G$, the <identity element> is $H=eH$, and $(gH)^{-1}=g^{-1}H$. These verify the <group axioms>.

<Lagrange's theorem> counts the equal-sized cosets:
$$
\boxed{|G|=|G/H|\,|H|,\qquad |H|\mid |G|.}
$$
Finally, if $|G|=p$ is <prime>, choose $g\ne e$. Its <cyclic subgroup> has order greater than one dividing $p$, hence equals $G$. All elements are powers of $g$, so $g^jg^k=g^{j+k}=g^kg^j$. Thus
$$
\boxed{G\cong C_p\text{ and }G\text{ is abelian}.}
$$

= d
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Write a matrix in the set as $M(a,b)$. The zero matrix $M(0,0)$ has no multiplicative <inverse matrix>, so $S$ is **not a group**. Its <determinant> is
$$
\det M(a,b)=|a|^2+|b|^2,
$$
which vanishes only at that zero matrix. It is therefore the only element that needs to be removed.

For closure, direct <matrix multiplication> gives
$$
M(a,b)M(c,d)=M(ac-bd^*,\ ad+bc^*).
$$
The product of nonzero determinants is nonzero, so two nonzero elements never multiply to zero. The <identity matrix> is $M(1,0)$, <associativity> is inherited from matrix multiplication, and
$$
\boxed{M(a,b)^{-1}=\frac1{|a|^2+|b|^2}\begin{pmatrix}a^*&-b\\b^*&a\end{pmatrix}
=M\left(\frac{a^*}{|a|^2+|b|^2},\frac{-b}{|a|^2+|b|^2}\right).}
$$
Thus $\boxed{S\setminus\{0_{2\times2}\}\text{ is a group}}$. It is the multiplicative group of nonzero <quaternions> in the <complex matrix representation of quaternions>.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
