= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Act on the four labelled vertices. A proper rotation gives an even <permutation>, identifying the rotational <tetrahedral symmetry> group with $A_4$, an <alternating group>. Geometrically there are one identity, three half-turns about opposite-edge midpoint axes, and two nontrivial rotations about each of four vertex-face axes. Therefore
$$
\boxed{|G|=1+3+8=12.}
$$
The <tetrahedral rotation conjugacy classes> are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|l|c}
&\text{elements in vertex notation}&\text{size}\\\hline
C_0&\{e\}&1\\
C_2&\{(12)(34),(13)(24),(14)(23)\}&3\\
C_+&\{(123),(134),(142),(243)\}&4\\
C_-&\{(132),(143),(124),(234)\}&4
\end{array}}
$$
To justify the splitting, the <centralizer> of a 3-cycle within $A_4$ is its three-element <cyclic subgroup>, so its <conjugacy class> has size $12/3=4$. Its inverse is not conjugate to it in $A_4$: the permutations that reverse that cycle while preserving its fixed vertex are odd. Conjugating $(123)$ by the even permutations produces the displayed $C_+$, and taking inverses gives $C_-$. The three half-turns are conjugate, since rotations move their axes into one another. Their centralizer has order four and their class therefore has size three.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let
$$
K=\{e,(12)(34),(13)(24),(14)(23)\}.
$$
The product of any two distinct nonidentity elements is the third, and each squares to $e$, so $K$ is a <Klein four-group>. Conjugating a double transposition simply relabels its letters, keeping it in the set. Hence $K$ is a <normal subgroup> of $A_4$.

Choose $r=(123)$. The <cosets> $K,rK,r^2K$ are distinct and cover the twelve elements. With $\zeta=e^{2\pi i/3}$, define
$$
\boxed{\Phi(r^jk)=\zeta^j,\qquad j\in\{0,1,2\},\quad k\in K.}
$$
The coset decomposition makes this well-defined. Normality gives
$$
(r^jk)(r^\ell k')=r^{j+\ell}(r^{-\ell}kr^\ell)k'\in r^{j+\ell}K,
$$
so $\Phi$ preserves products. It is onto $\{1,\zeta,\zeta^2\}\cong C_3$, and
$$
\boxed{\ker\Phi=K,\qquad A_4/K\cong C_3.}
$$
This is the <tetrahedral quotient onto the cyclic group of order three>. In terms of (a), it sends $C_0\cup C_2$ to $1$, $C_+$ to $\zeta$, and $C_-$ to $\zeta^2$. The normality check above also follows abstractly because a <group homomorphism kernel> is normal.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The group is the <direct product of groups> $\mathbb Z_p\times\mathbb Z_q$. Repeatedly adding $(1,1)$ returns to $(0,0)$ for the first time when both $p$ and $q$ divide the step number. Its <order of a group element> is therefore $\operatorname{lcm}(p,q)$. If $p,q$ are <coprime>, this is $pq$, so it generates every element:
$$
\boxed{G(p,q)\cong C_{pq}.}
$$
Explicitly, $[t]_{pq}\mapsto([t]_p,[t]_q)$ is the <Chinese remainder theorem> <group isomorphism>.

If $d=\gcd(p,q)>1$, every pair satisfies
$$
\frac{pq}{d}(n,m)=(0,0).
$$
Thus every element order divides $pq/d<pq$, and no element can generate the whole group. Hence
$$
\boxed{G(p,q)\text{ is cyclic exactly when }\gcd(p,q)=1.}
$$
This is the <cyclicity of a product of two finite cyclic groups>.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
