= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The upper-left block of $U$ is the planar <rotation matrix> $R(\pi/2)$, and $U$ fixes the third coordinate. Therefore
$$
U^p=\begin{pmatrix}\cos(p\pi/2)&-\sin(p\pi/2)&0\\\sin(p\pi/2)&\cos(p\pi/2)&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad U^4=I.
$$
The diagonal entries of $V$ square to one, so $V^2=I$. Direct conjugation gives $VUV=U^{-1}$, hence $VU^pV=U^{-p}$ for every integer $p$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{U^pV=VU^{-p}=VU^{4-(p\bmod4)}.}
$$
When $p\bmod4=0$, the last exponent is four, which is equivalent to zero because $U^4=I$.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The relations in (a) reduce every word in the generators to $U^aV^b$, with $0\leq a<4$ and $b\in\{0,1\}$. These eight <matrices> are distinct: the $b=0$ elements have third diagonal entry $1$ and the $b=1$ elements have entry $-1$, while the four planar blocks are distinct in each family. Their multiplication is
$$
\boxed{(U^aV^b)(U^cV^d)=U^{a+(-1)^bc}V^{b+d},}
$$
with exponents reduced modulo four and two. This proves closure. The <identity element> is $U^0V^0$, the inverse of $U^a$ is $U^{-a}$, and each $U^aV$ is its own inverse. <Associativity> is inherited from matrix multiplication, proving the <group axioms>. This is the eight-element <dihedral group> $D_8$.

Set $g_j=U^j$ and $g_{j+4}=U^jV$ for $j=0,1,2,3$, consistently with the two labels specified in the question. The row-times-column <multiplication table> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
&g_0&g_1&g_2&g_3&g_4&g_5&g_6&g_7\\\hline
g_0&g_0&g_1&g_2&g_3&g_4&g_5&g_6&g_7\\
g_1&g_1&g_2&g_3&g_0&g_5&g_6&g_7&g_4\\
g_2&g_2&g_3&g_0&g_1&g_6&g_7&g_4&g_5\\
g_3&g_3&g_0&g_1&g_2&g_7&g_4&g_5&g_6\\
g_4&g_4&g_7&g_6&g_5&g_0&g_3&g_2&g_1\\
g_5&g_5&g_4&g_7&g_6&g_1&g_0&g_3&g_2\\
g_6&g_6&g_5&g_4&g_7&g_2&g_1&g_0&g_3\\
g_7&g_7&g_6&g_5&g_4&g_3&g_2&g_1&g_0
\end{array}}
$$
The rotation powers commute with one another, and $U^a$ commutes with $U^cV$ exactly when $a$ is even. Two elements $U^aV,U^cV$ commute exactly when $a-c$ is even. These statements also check the symmetric portions of the table.

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <character of a representation> is the <trace> of its representing matrix. For the rotation powers,
$$
\chi(U^j)=1+2\cos(j\pi/2).
$$
Each $U^jV$ has a planar reflection block of trace zero and third diagonal entry $-1$, giving character $-1$. In the chosen element order,
$$
\boxed{(\chi(g_0),\ldots,\chi(g_7))=(3,1,-1,1,-1,-1,-1,-1).}
$$
Equivalently its values on the <conjugacy classes> $\{I\}$, $\{U^2\}$, $\{U,U^3\}$, $\{V,U^2V\}$, $\{UV,U^3V\}$ are $3,-1,1,-1,-1$.

= d
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The complete <subgroup> list is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{l|c|c}
\text{subgroup}&\text{order}&\text{isomorphism type}\\\hline
\{I\}&1&C_1\\
\{I,U^2\}&2&C_2\\
\{I,U^jV\},\quad j=0,1,2,3&2&C_2\\
\{I,U,U^2,U^3\}&4&C_4\\
\{I,U^2,V,U^2V\}&4&C_2\times C_2\\
\{I,U^2,UV,U^3V\}&4&C_2\times C_2\\
G&8&D_8
\end{array}}
$$
Thus there are ten <subgroups>: one trivial, five of order two, three of order four, and the whole group. The five order-two subgroups are mutually isomorphic. The two noncyclic order-four subgroups are mutually isomorphic <Klein four-groups>; neither is isomorphic to the <cyclic group of order four>.

For completeness, <Lagrange's theorem> permits only orders $1,2,4,8$. An order-four subgroup containing $U$ or $U^3$ must be $\langle U\rangle$. Otherwise it can contain only $I,U^2$ and elements $U^jV$. The product of two distinct such elements is $U^{j-k}$. To avoid generating $U$ or $U^3$, their exponents must differ by two, giving exactly the two listed <Klein four-groups>. The order-two subgroups come from the five <involutions> $U^2$ and $U^jV$. This proves completeness.

= e
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For any <cyclic subgroup> $H=\langle s\rangle$ of order $n\in\{1,2,4\}$, define
$$
\boxed{\rho_1(s^j)=e^{2\pi ij/n}\in GL(1,\mathbb C),\qquad
\rho_2(s^j)=\begin{pmatrix}\cos(2\pi j/n)&-\sin(2\pi j/n)\\\sin(2\pi j/n)&\cos(2\pi j/n)\end{pmatrix}\in GL(2,\mathbb R).}
$$
Both preserve products because scalar phases and planar rotations add their angles. Each sends the generator to an element of exact order $n$, so both have trivial <kernel> and are <faithful representations>.

For $\langle U\rangle\cong C_4$, take $s=U$: the generator images are $i$ and $R(\pi/2)$. For each order-two subgroup take its nonidentity element $s$; the images are $-1$ and $-I_2$. For the <trivial subgroup> the unique images are $1$ and $I_2$, which are faithful on a one-element group.

The one-dimensional representation of $C_4$ here must use complex scalars. There is **no faithful one-dimensional real representation of $C_4$**, since a nonzero real scalar of finite order is $1$ or $-1$, and neither has order four. The two-dimensional representations above are real.
